From: KRdp displayless experiment <noreply@example.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] Take key repeat from the RDP client, as on a Windows server

mstsc sends key repeats as further key-downs of a held key. KRdp forwarded them
as presses, which KWin drops as duplicates, so held keys repeated on the server
side instead, and a key-up delayed by a slow link left the server repeating:
letters multiplied by the dozen. With KRDP_CLIENT_KEY_REPEAT set - meaning the
deployment has turned server-side repeat off - each client repeat becomes its
own keystroke, a release followed by a press. Repeats of modifiers and lock keys
are dropped.

This scoped version relies on the session having no local keyboard. The
in-code comment records the device-scoped design an upstream proposal would
need instead.

diff --git a/src/InputHandler.cpp b/src/InputHandler.cpp
index f3d9652..f5ac8c7 100644
--- a/src/InputHandler.cpp
+++ b/src/InputHandler.cpp
@@ -4,6 +4,7 @@
 
 #include "InputHandler.h"
 
+#include <linux/input-event-codes.h>
 #include <QKeyEvent>
 #include <QMetaObject>
 #include <QSet>
@@ -216,6 +217,69 @@ bool InputHandler::keyboardEvent(uint16_t code, uint16_t flags)
 
     auto type = flags & KBD_FLAGS_RELEASE ? QEvent::KeyRelease : QEvent::KeyPress;
 
+    // Key repeat, the way an RDP server on Windows does it: repeats come from
+    // the client only. mstsc sends each repeat as another key-down of a key
+    // that is still held, flagged KBD_FLAGS_DOWN. Without this they are
+    // forwarded as presses, which KWin drops as duplicates, so held keys
+    // repeat on the server side instead - and when the key-up is late on a
+    // slow link, the server keeps repeating and letters multiply. With
+    // KRDP_CLIENT_KEY_REPEAT set, the deployment has turned server-side repeat
+    // off, and each client repeat becomes a keystroke of its own here: a
+    // release followed by a press, which every kind of Wayland or Xwayland
+    // client handles. Repeats of modifiers and lock keys are dropped, so a
+    // held Shift or Ctrl stays held and Caps Lock does not toggle on repeat.
+    //
+    // Known limitation: an application that watches whether a non-modifier
+    // key stays down (hold Space to pan, games) sees it flicker at the
+    // client's repeat rate. That is the price of not touching KWin.
+    //
+    // Upstream shape, if this is ever proposed. The version here relies on
+    // the session having no local keyboard; turning off repeat for a whole
+    // seat would break one in a shared session. A proper fix scopes it to
+    // the remote device instead, across three projects:
+    //   - plasma-wayland-protocols: a "repeated" key state for
+    //     org_kde_kwin_fake_input, plus an opt-in meaning "this device
+    //     delivers its own repeats", so other fake-input users keep today's
+    //     behaviour; a protocol version bump.
+    //   - KWin: for an opted-in device, do not start the seat's repeat timer
+    //     (KeyboardRepeat::keyboardKey would need the device passed in), and
+    //     deliver its repeated events as wl_keyboard v10 REPEATED to clients
+    //     that support it - Qt 6.11 does - with no fake key-ups. Clients
+    //     below v10, Xwayland among them, repeat by themselves from one
+    //     seat-wide rate that cannot be scoped per device; give those this
+    //     device's keys as discrete taps, so they never see a key held long
+    //     enough to start repeating. Local keyboards keep the seat's repeat.
+    //   - KRdp: send client repeats as "repeated" and opt the device in.
+    // The portal path injects through libei, whose key events are press or
+    // release only, so it would need an equivalent extension in libei and
+    // in KWin's EIS side, or the MR has to be scoped to fake-input. Worth
+    // checking first: which clients - Xwayland, Chromium/Electron - support
+    // wl_keyboard v10, since that decides how much the tap fallback matters.
+    static const bool clientKeyRepeat = qEnvironmentVariableIntValue("KRDP_CLIENT_KEY_REPEAT") != 0;
+    const bool isRepeat = type == QEvent::KeyPress && (d->pressedKeys.contains(keycode) || (flags & KBD_FLAGS_DOWN));
+    if (clientKeyRepeat && isRepeat) {
+        switch (keycode) {
+        case KEY_LEFTSHIFT:
+        case KEY_RIGHTSHIFT:
+        case KEY_LEFTCTRL:
+        case KEY_RIGHTCTRL:
+        case KEY_LEFTALT:
+        case KEY_RIGHTALT:
+        case KEY_LEFTMETA:
+        case KEY_RIGHTMETA:
+        case KEY_CAPSLOCK:
+        case KEY_NUMLOCK:
+        case KEY_SCROLLLOCK:
+            return true;
+        default:
+            break;
+        }
+        d->pressedKeys.insert(keycode);
+        Q_EMIT inputEvent(std::make_shared<QKeyEvent>(QEvent::KeyRelease, 0, Qt::KeyboardModifiers{}, keycode, 0, 0));
+        Q_EMIT inputEvent(std::make_shared<QKeyEvent>(QEvent::KeyPress, 0, Qt::KeyboardModifiers{}, keycode, 0, 0));
+        return true;
+    }
+
     if (type == QEvent::KeyRelease) {
         d->pressedKeys.remove(keycode);
     } else {
